WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.323
Where the tokens go.

2
00:00:01.603 --> 00:00:05.933
Open any request your system sends and sort its tokens into six buckets.

3
00:00:06.213 --> 00:00:07.749
Each one has a different fix.

4
00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:09.117
System prompt.

5
00:00:09.397 --> 00:00:11.744
Instructions, policies, examples.

6
00:00:12.024 --> 00:00:16.547
Stable across requests, so it is the natural thing to cache (lesson 5).

7
00:00:16.827 --> 00:00:18.085
Tool definitions.

8
00:00:18.365 --> 00:00:21.288
Every schema you attach is input on every call.

9
00:00:21.568 --> 00:00:35.179
On top of your schemas, the API adds a tool-use system prompt whenever tools are present; the pricing page lists 286 tokens for the current Opus, Sonnet, and Haiku models with automatic tool choice.

10
00:00:35.459 --> 00:00:38.104
Small per request, large across millions.

11
00:00:38.384 --> 00:00:39.216
History.

12
00:00:39.496 --> 00:00:44.787
The API is stateless, so a multi-turn conversation resends every earlier turn.

13
00:00:45.067 --> 00:00:47.349
This is the bucket that surprises people.

14
00:00:47.629 --> 00:00:48.717
Tool results.

15
00:00:48.997 --> 00:00:51.771
Search hits, file contents, database rows.

16
00:00:52.051 --> 00:00:55.165
Sent once, then reread on every later turn of the loop.

17
00:00:55.445 --> 00:00:56.256
Thinking.

18
00:00:56.536 --> 00:01:04.472
Reasoning tokens are billed as output tokens even when the thinking text isn’t returned to you, according to Anthropic’s thinking docs.

19
00:01:04.752 --> 00:01:08.251
A short visible answer can carry a long, invisible bill.

20
00:01:08.531 --> 00:01:09.661
Visible output.

21
00:01:09.941 --> 00:01:13.163
The answer, plus any tool-call arguments the model writes.

22
00:01:13.443 --> 00:01:18.477
The history bucket deserves a closer look, because it changes the shape of the cost curve.

23
00:01:18.757 --> 00:01:28.357
Suppose an agent’s prefix (system prompt plus tools) is P tokens and each turn appends about d tokens (the model’s reply and a tool result).

24
00:01:28.637 --> 00:01:30.941
Turn k sends P plus k times d.

25
00:01:31.221 --> 00:01:39.243
Over n turns the total input is n times P plus d times n(n minus 1)/2, which grows like n squared.

26
00:01:39.523 --> 00:01:45.965
Anthropic’s cost guide says the same thing in words: a 40-turn task sends its first turn 40 times.

27
00:01:46.245 --> 00:01:56.165
Caching doesn’t stop the resending, but it reprices the already-seen part at the cache-read rate, which is why caching is the largest single cost lever for agents.
